# Number of files under targets/esp32-idf/ that may include the Arduino shim
# (arduino_compat.h, or Arduino.h which forwards to it).
#
# This number may only ever go DOWN. Raising it is the failure mode the ratchet exists to
# prevent -- see ratchet.sh and docs/MIGRATION.md § "The ESP32 import is different".
#
# Lower it with each subsystem that stops needing the shim.
#
# THE ENDSTATE IS "ONLY THE VENDOR ADAPTER REMAINS" (decided 2026-08-04), not an empty
# directory. FastEPD needs a PERMANENT compatibility layer -- it is a vendored Arduino library
# and there is no version of it that does not want Arduino primitives. So when this count
# reaches 0, the surviving pieces (delay(), delayMicroseconds(), millis() with C++ linkage,
# ledc_compat.h) are EXTRACTED into the vendor adapter and owned there; only what is left over
# is deleted, along with the ratchet and its workflow.
#
# It is called an ADAPTER and not a shim on purpose. In this repo "shim" means scheduled
# demolition -- that is the whole premise of this file -- and using the word for something
# permanent would make the ratchet's own vocabulary meaningless.
#
# The count itself can still reach 0: the last three entries (buzzer_hw.cpp,
# device_control.cpp, encryption.cpp) are counted only for their TARGET_NRF arms, which leave
# at MIGRATION step 4. Reaching 0 is the trigger for the extraction, not a licence to delete.
#
# 21 = the phase-B baseline. It was 20 when the metric counted only arduino_compat.h/Arduino.h;
# widening it to every shim header (Wire, SPI, WiFi, ledc, esp32-hal-gpio) found one more file.
# The shim did not grow -- the measurement got honest. Every entry is a file that must
# eventually either move to shared/core (which forbids Arduino outright) or be rewritten
# against IDF and shared/hal directly.
#
# 22 = the same thing happening a second time, 2026-07-26, when the remaining ESP32 boards
# were added. HardwareSerial.h was missing from the pattern, and src/main.cpp has included it
# since the import -- inside the OPENDISPLAY_LOG_UART guard, which no board fragment defined
# until the *-extuart boards existed, so the header did not even exist to be counted. main.cpp
# is arguably the most Arduino-dependent file in the target and it was scoring zero.
#
# THE SHIM DID NOT GROW EITHER TIME. Both bumps are the metric catching up with reality, and
# both are recorded here rather than quietly applied, because "we widened the definition" and
# "we let it grow" are indistinguishable from the number alone -- and a ratchet that cannot
# tell them apart is not a ratchet. Any future bump needs the same paragraph, or it is the
# failure this file exists to prevent.
#
# 21 = the first REAL decrease, 2026-08-02, during the sync to Firmware
# feat/psram-dram-reclaim. The sync deleted three shim users (ble_init.cpp, ble_init.h and
# esp32_ble_callbacks.h, replaced by the BleTransport seam, plus ble/od_ble_rx.cpp whose job
# moved into the shared command_queue.cpp) and brought in four new ones. Left alone that would
# have been 22 -> 25 and the first genuine growth. It was fixed rather than bumped, which is
# what this file is for:
#
#   * src/ble_transport_esp32.cpp  -- written for this target during the sync, so there was
#                                     never a reason for it to be on the shim. One delay()
#                                     -> vTaskDelay().
#   * src/link_owner.cpp           -- <Arduino.h> for millis() ALONE. It is also the clearest
#                                     promotion candidate in the target (pure arbitration
#                                     logic, no vendor surface, already has a host test), and
#                                     shared/ forbids Arduino outright -- so the dependency had
#                                     to go before the promotion, not after it.
#   * src/session_guard.cpp        -- <Arduino.h> for millis() and one delay().
#   * src/utilities/nrf52840_reformat/ -- an nRF52840 standalone flash-recovery program with
#                                     its own setup()/loop(), pulling in Adafruit_TinyUSB and
#                                     nrf.h. It is not compiled by this target and never will
#                                     be; it arrived only because the sync copies src/
#                                     wholesale. Dropped rather than counted: CLAUDE.md is
#                                     explicit that the Nordic half leaves at step 4 for
#                                     targets/nordic-zephyr/, and a directory under
#                                     targets/esp32-idf/ means "this target builds it".
#
# The first three are marked OD-PORT at the call sites so the next sync from Firmware can see
# why those lines differ from the reference tree.
#
# 20 = phase C step 1, 2026-08-04: the logger. src/od_log.h stopped including <Arduino.h>.
#
# It was picked first because it is the keystone, not because it is the smallest. od_log.h is
# included by nearly every file in the target, so for as long as it pulled in <Arduino.h> it
# was handing the Arduino surface to files that did not ask for it -- and any of those could
# have "dropped the shim" by deleting its own include while still compiling on od_log.h's.
# That is the laundering this file exists to catch, and the only way to stop it is at the
# source. Six files include od_log.h without a shim header of their own; five are already
# deliberately Arduino-free (link_owner, session_guard, command_queue, both ble_transports),
# which is what made the blast radius small enough to go first.
#
# The only Arduino thing in the logger was the `Stream *` it was handed. A Stream is a PORT,
# not policy, so the port moved to hal/od_hal_log.{h,c} -- opened by compile-time board choice
# (IDF UART driver for the *-extuart boards, stdout for USB-CDC) -- and od_log.cpp's `Stream *`
# became a plain bool. On nRF it had ALREADY been nothing but an initialised flag: the record
# bytes there go out through tud_cdc_write(), never through the Stream.
#
# compat/HardwareSerial.h is DELETED with this step: main.cpp's OPENDISPLAY_LOG_UART path was
# its only user, and that code is now od_hal_log's UART backend. The shim lost a whole header,
# not just a caller.
#
# ONE FILE MOVED THE OTHER WAY, and it is the honest part. main.cpp lost <HardwareSerial.h>
# and GAINED an explicit <Arduino.h>, so it stays counted. It still calls Serial.*, delay(),
# pinMode() and digitalWrite(), and has always got them transitively through main.h -- so
# letting the HardwareSerial removal take it off the list would have scored a decrement for a
# file that is arguably the most Arduino-dependent in the target. 21 -> 20, one file, not two.
#
# Note for the endgame: delay(), delayMicroseconds() and millis() are shimmed with C++ linkage
# and defined out-of-line in arduino_compat.cpp because two VENDORED libraries need them
# externally (bb_epaper.h declares `void delay(long)` unmangled; FastEPD's arduino_io.inl has
# an extern millis() with 19 call sites and no definition). They are pinned by third_party/,
# not by app code.
#
# As of 2026-08-04 that is a DECISION rather than an obstacle: FastEPD keeps a permanent
# compatibility layer, so these three plus ledc_compat.h become the vendor adapter. ratchet.sh's
# report-only third-party list is that adapter's specification -- read it as a definition of
# what must survive, not as a list of things to eliminate.
#
# 19 = phase C step 2, 2026-08-04: src/factory_config.cpp. A one-line removal, and recorded as
# what it is -- bookkeeping that step 1 made TRUE, not work in its own right.
#
# The file uses no Arduino API at all. Its `#include <Arduino.h>` has been dead since the
# import. Removing it BEFORE step 1 would have been the laundered decrement this file keeps
# warning about: the include would have gone, the file would still have compiled, and it would
# have compiled on the Arduino that od_log.h was dragging in behind it -- so the number would
# have fallen while nothing changed. After step 1 the whole chain (factory_config.h ->
# config_parser.h -> stdint.h, communication.h, od_log.h) is Arduino-free, so the build itself
# is now the proof, which is the only reason this counts.
#
# The general rule this is the first instance of: a file whose only Arduino dependency was
# transitive cannot be cleared until the header that supplied it is cleared. Expect more of
# these to fall out for free as the real subsystems land -- and expect none of them to be
# claimable before that.
#
# 17 = phase C step 3, 2026-08-04: the power latch and the wake-button armer, and with them the
# GPIO/clock seam the rest of the leaf files will reuse -- hal/od_hal_gpio.{h,c} and
# hal/od_hal_time.{h,c}, signatures taken from docs/SHARED_API_DESIGN.md rather than invented.
#
# TWO FILES IN ONE STEP, which MIGRATION.md's "do not batch subsystem swaps" allows only
# because they are one subsystem: power_latch.cpp owns pwr_pin_2/pwr_pin_3, and wake_button.cpp
# exists partly to refuse to arm those same two pins (arming the latch clock would clock the
# rail off mid-sleep). Splitting them would have put a commit boundary through one hardware
# interlock. They revert together for the same reason.
#
# wake_button.cpp was ONE LINE -- a digitalRead. The file already included driver/gpio.h and
# was otherwise pure IDF (esp_sleep_*), and the shim's digitalRead was itself gpio_get_level
# behind a validity guard, so it calls gpio_get_level directly rather than taking a HAL
# dependency it does not need. Not every leaf needs the seam.
#
# power_latch.cpp is the real one: 11 pinMode, 9 digitalWrite, 4 delayMicroseconds, 2
# digitalRead, 2 millis, 2 delay. Two notes on the translation, both about not changing
# hardware behaviour by accident:
#
#   * pinMode(p, OUTPUT) + digitalWrite(p, X) collapses to od_hal_gpio_config_output(p, X),
#     which is gpio_config() THEN gpio_set_level() -- the same two register writes in the same
#     order. It is deliberately not one "configure at level" call: gpio_config() does not touch
#     the output latch, so a merged version would change the glitch behaviour on a pin that was
#     already driven the other way. On this file's hardware that glitch cuts the rail.
#   * od_hal_delay_ms() keeps the shim's round-UP-to-whole-ticks arithmetic. At the default
#     100 Hz tick a plain vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(5)) rounds to zero and returns immediately,
#     turning a settle into a busy-spin.
#
# NOT hardware-verified, and this is the step where that matters most so far: the latch is the
# path that can leave a unit unable to power on. Build-verified on five boards across four
# chips only.
#
# 16 = phase C step 4, 2026-08-04: the buzzer. TWO files were converted, but only ONE leaves
# the count -- src/buzzer_control.cpp. Read the rest of this entry before assuming that is a
# mistake.
#
# src/buzzer_hw.cpp's ESP32 half is now Arduino-free: it drives IDF's ledc driver directly and
# calls od_hal_gpio for the park-low. But the file also carries an ARDUINO_ARCH_NRF52 arm
# (Bluefruit HardwarePWM) that needs pinMode/digitalWrite, so <Arduino.h> is still in the file
# and the ratchet still counts it. That is CORRECT, and is left alone deliberately:
#
#   * the grep is textual by design -- what it measures is "files that still name Arduino",
#     and this one does;
#   * the alternative was declaring pinMode/digitalWrite rather than including Arduino, the
#     trick od_log.cpp uses for millis(). Rejected here: that arm's linkage cannot be checked
#     from this repo, and guessing wrong buys a decrement at the price of a link error on a
#     target nobody can build yet;
#   * deleting the nRF arm outright would score the decrement honestly, but it is a real
#     divergence from the reference tree inside a commit about removing Arduino, and it makes
#     the next sync conflict. That arm dies at migration step 4, where it is rewritten for
#     Zephyr anyway.
#
# A LATENT DEFECT FELL OUT OF THIS, and it is why the step was worth doing carefully.
# buzzer_hw.cpp forked on `#if ESP_ARDUINO_VERSION_MAJOR >= 3`: a 3.x pin-based LEDC path and a
# 2.x channel-based one. That macro is defined NOWHERE under IDF, so the test was `0 >= 3` and
# this target had silently been compiling the 2.x branch -- the one the reference firmware, on
# Arduino core 3.x, does not take. compat/ledc_compat.h implements only the 2.x calls, which
# corroborates it: the 3.x branch could never have linked.
#
# Consequence, now fixed: the 2.x branch computed the frequency as a double and handed it to a
# uint32_t shim parameter, so it TRUNCATED where 3.x rounds -- up to 1 Hz flat, worst at the
# bottom of the range. The fork is gone rather than resolved toward one side: there is one LEDC
# peripheral and one way to drive it from IDF, and a version macro from a framework this target
# no longer uses cannot select anything meaningful. Timer/channel selection is deliberately
# UNCHANGED, oddities included (channel 7, `7 % LEDC_TIMER_MAX`, `7 % LEDC_CHANNEL_MAX`, which
# land differently per chip), because that is what is flashed today.
#
# compat/ledc_compat.h SURVIVES. Its last app-code user just left, but FastEPD.inl's
# front-light control uses ledcSetup/ledcAttachPin/ledcWrite at 11 sites. It is the third thing
# now pinned by third_party/ rather than by our code, after delay() and millis().

# 14 = phase C step 5, 2026-08-04: the I2C sensor drivers -- src/sensor_sht40.cpp and
# src/sensor_bq27220.cpp -- onto a new hal/od_hal_i2c.{h,c}.
#
# THE SEAM HAD TO TAKE THE BUS WITH IT, and that is the interesting part. IDF permits exactly
# one i2c_new_master_bus() per port. compat/Wire.h owned that handle, and touch_input.cpp
# (step 6) and display_service.cpp (step 10, the AXP2101 PMIC plus the whole bus lifecycle)
# still drive Wire. Converting the sensors to a HAL that opened its own bus would have meant
# two owners of one port -- which does not fail cleanly, it fails wherever the two disagree
# about who configured the pins.
#
# So od_hal_i2c owns the bus and compat/Wire.h became a thin ADAPTER over it. The bus
# mechanics moved rather than being rewritten: same IDF calls, same timeouts, same
# device-handle cache. What stayed in Wire.h is only the Arduino semantics -- the buffered
# beginTransmission/write/endTransmission idiom and the 0/1/2/3/4 return encoding, which is
# the INVERSE of both esp_err_t and od_hal_i2c's, and is translated at that boundary and
# nowhere else.
#
# TWO HARDWARE-FOUND BEHAVIOURS CARRIED ACROSS DELIBERATELY. Both were already fixed in
# Wire.h; neither is obvious from the call sites, and either would be easy to lose in a
# rewrite:
#
#   * A ZERO-LENGTH WRITE IS A PRESENCE PROBE. IDF's i2c_master_transmit() rejects size 0
#     outright, so a scan built on it never reaches the bus -- no START, no address byte, no
#     ACK to observe -- and reports every address absent whether or not hardware is there. A
#     connected SHT40 was undetectable. od_hal_i2c_probe() is the address-only primitive.
#   * endTransmission(false) MUST NOT SEND A STOP. The BQ27220 register read is one
#     transaction with a repeated START; split into STOP + fresh START it answers as if
#     unaddressed, so the gauge returns plausible garbage rather than an error. That is
#     od_hal_i2c_write_read().
#
# THE HAL'S SHAPE DIVERGES FROM docs/SHARED_API_DESIGN.md, and this is flagged rather than
# quietly done. That sketch is register-oriented -- read(addr, reg, buf, len) -- and cannot
# express either driver: the SHT40 measurement is write-command, STOP, wait ~9 ms, then a bare
# read (no register, and the delay must sit between two transactions), while the BQ27220 is a
# single repeated-START transaction. A register-shaped call gets the bus framing wrong for
# each, in opposite directions. Primitives here; the doc's two calls are expressible on top,
# the reverse is not. The sketch was written from reading, and this is what building it found.

# 13 = phase C step 6, 2026-08-04: src/touch_input.cpp, the GT911 driver. It reuses step 5's
# od_hal_i2c and brings the GPIO interrupt surface into hal/od_hal_gpio -- config_irq(),
# clear_irq(), irq_lock()/unlock(), and set_mode_output().
#
# TWO PLACES WHERE docs/SHARED_API_DESIGN.md's SKETCH WAS TOO NARROW, both widened
# deliberately and both for a reason the hardware supplies:
#
#   * config_irq() is specified as edge-both with no mode argument. The GT911 asserts INT
#     active-low and is attached FALLING; edge-both would raise a spurious event on every
#     release. The HAL takes an od_hal_gpio_edge_t.
#   * set_mode_output() is not in the sketch at all, and exists because pinMode() and
#     digitalWrite() are SEPARABLE and gt911_hw_reset() depends on the separation. It makes
#     both INT and RST outputs BEFORE driving either, and INT's level at RST's rising edge is
#     what selects the controller's I2C address (0x14 vs 0x5D). Collapsing those pairs into
#     config_output(), which is what every other call site got, would reorder the sequence and
#     change which address the part answers on. The whole reset function is therefore
#     transcribed call-for-call rather than tidied.
#
# ONE FUNCTION DELETED, and it is worth knowing why it was not a bus operation.
# gt911_drain_wire() was `while (Wire.available()) Wire.read();`, called on every I2C retry.
# It drained bytes left in the SHIM's RX buffer after a partial read -- an artifact of
# Arduino's requestFrom()/read() split, with nothing corresponding on the wire. od_hal_i2c
# transfers are all-or-nothing, so there is no residue and nothing for it to do. Its call
# sites read like bus recovery and never were.
#
# The read path keeps BOTH framings on purpose: gt911_read_reg() tries the repeated-START form
# and then the STOP-then-START form, because GT911 clones differ in which they accept. That is
# the second consumer of od_hal_i2c exposing write_read() and write()+read() separately rather
# than one register-shaped call.
#
# NOT hardware-verified, and touch is the subsystem where that is hardest to reason about from
# a build: an address-selection sequence that comes out wrong produces a controller that simply
# never answers, which looks identical to absent hardware.

# 12 = phase C step 7, 2026-08-04: src/device_control.cpp -- buttons, LEDs, the ADC ladder and
# DFU entry -- plus a new hal/od_hal_adc.{h,c} and the rest of the GPIO interrupt surface.
#
# ONE FILE LEAVES THE COUNT, NOT TWO, and the same rule as buzzer_hw.cpp is why.
# device_control.h drops <Arduino.h>, which it used for uint8_t alone, and leaves.
# device_control.cpp's ESP32 half is now entirely od_hal_adc/od_hal_gpio/od_hal_time -- its
# only shim header, esp32-hal-gpio.h, is gone -- but its TARGET_NRF arm still calls
# pinMode/attachInterrupt/detachInterrupt, so it GAINS a guarded <Arduino.h> and stays counted.
# 13 -> 12. The alternative was a decrement for a file that still names Arduino, which is the
# laundering this file exists to catch.
#
# hal/od_hal_adc is NOT in docs/SHARED_API_DESIGN.md at all -- the core does not read analogue
# inputs. It exists because two TARGET drivers do (this file's button ladder and
# display_service.cpp's battery sense) and IDF's oneshot driver has ONE ADC1 unit handle:
# adc_oneshot_new_unit() on an already-open unit fails, so two owners would mean whichever
# initialised second silently read nothing. Same forced-consolidation as step 5's I2C bus. The
# shim's analogRead/analogSetPinAttenuation/analogReadResolution are now forwarders, because
# display_service.cpp still calls them until step 10.
#
# WHY THAT MATTERS MORE HERE THAN IT LOOKS. Both callers turn a reading into something a host
# believes. readBatteryVoltageUncached() scales it and publishes it in the MSD advert, so 0
# becomes a plausible 0.0 V rather than the -1.0 "unknown" the same function returns when no
# sense pin is configured. The ladder classifies 0 into its catch-all bottom bucket, which
# reads as the last button permanently pressed. That is exactly what the original `return 0`
# ADC stub produced, and it is why od_hal_adc exposes pin_readable() as a separate question
# instead of leaving callers to infer failure from a value that is also a legal reading. The
# ladder now warns once at registration when its pin is not ADC1-capable.
#
# ADC1 ONLY, carried across deliberately: ADC2 shares hardware with the WiFi radio on
# ESP32/S2/S3 and reads fail with ESP_ERR_TIMEOUT whenever WiFi is up, which these boards run.
#
# The GPIO HAL gained config_irq_arg() -- Arduino's attachInterruptArg, which is what lets four
# buttons share one ISR instead of the four near-identical ones touch_input.cpp was stuck with
# -- and irq_enable/irq_disable, which MASK without detaching. That distinction is load-bearing
# for this file: its button re-baselining disables every button interrupt, settles, re-reads the
# pins and re-enables. Detaching instead would discard each handler AND its index argument, and
# re-attaching is where a wrong index lands one button's events on its neighbour.
#
# One simplification worth naming, since it removes a state the pad used to pass through:
# `pinMode(pin, INPUT)` followed by a conditional `pinMode(pin, INPUT_PULLUP/PULLDOWN)` became
# a single od_hal_gpio_config_input(pin, pullup, pulldown). The intermediate pull-less
# configuration existed only because Arduino had no combined form. The pad ends in the same
# state.
#
# NOT hardware-verified. Buttons, LEDs, battery voltage and DFU entry all sit behind this.

# 11 = phase C step 8, 2026-08-04: src/encryption.h. The last Arduino String on an INTERFACE.
#
# `String getChipIdHex()` was the one remaining place Arduino's String appeared in a
# declaration rather than inside a function body, and CLAUDE.md forbids it in shared/ outright.
# It becomes `void getChipIdHex(char* out, size_t out_size)` with a stated contract: exactly six
# uppercase hex digits, zero-padded, NUL-terminated. Every caller was already padding to six by
# hand, so the contract replaces a convention that each site re-implemented.
#
# A TOO-SMALL BUFFER YIELDS AN EMPTY STRING, NOT A TRUNCATED ONE, and that is a deliberate
# choice rather than defensive habit: the host keys the device on its advertised name, so a
# shortened id is not a degraded id -- it is a DIFFERENT device. Empty fails visibly; truncated
# fails as a stranger appearing in Home Assistant.
#
# Three DEAD forward declarations went with it -- communication.cpp, display_service.cpp and
# ble_transport_nrf.cpp each declared the function and never called it.
#
# encryption.cpp's ESP32 half also came off the shim (millis, ESP.getEfuseMac, and
# checkResetPin's pinMode/digitalRead/delay), but the FILE stays counted: its TARGET_NRF arm
# still needs <Arduino.h> for random() and its own pinMode calls. Same rule as buzzer_hw.cpp and
# device_control.cpp. 12 -> 11, one file.
#
# ESP.getEfuseMac() -> esp_efuse_mac_get_default(), with the little-endian packing written out
# explicitly rather than tidied. The Arduino call returned the six factory bytes packed into a
# uint64_t with MAC byte 0 in the LOW byte, and both call sites shift that value to select a
# subrange. One feeds the AUTH device id and the other the advertised device NAME -- a
# different packing is a different device to the host, and the fleet is keyed on both.
#
# NOT hardware-verified. The device name and the AUTH device id both derive from the changed
# code, and getting either wrong presents as a new device rather than a broken one -- which is
# exactly the failure mode the S3 MAC pinning in sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3 exists to prevent.

# 9 = phase C step 9a, 2026-08-04: src/communication.cpp and src/config_parser.cpp -- the two
# small members of the WiFi/LAN group. wifi_service.cpp, the large one, is step 9b.
#
# NEITHER WAS REALLY A WiFi FILE, which is why they are worth separating from the step's
# headline. communication.cpp's shim dependency was 4 millis(), 3 delay() and one String; its
# <WiFi.h> had already gone in phase B, and the comment recording that is still there.
# config_parser.cpp's was a single WiFi.localIP().toString() for a status log line, plus
# <Arduino.h> for the same String.
#
# The String work is the part worth noting. handleFirmwareVersion() used three String
# operations -- strip surrounding quotes, trim, substitute a placeholder when empty -- on a
# build-stamped SHA that is at most 40 bytes on the wire, into a response array already sized
# for exactly that. Rewritten in place on a fixed buffer: the growable string was never buying
# anything, and communication.cpp is a dispatcher heading for shared/core, where String is
# forbidden outright.
#
# config_parser.cpp matters more than its one line suggests: it is the PRE-AUTH ATTACK SURFACE
# and the first planned shared/core promotion (NEXT_STEPS item 5). Neither IPAddress nor String
# may appear there, so the esp_netif call replaces a type round-trip that existed only to
# format four bytes.
#
# One build-only defect found here, and it is the kind a single-board build hides: the
# esp_netif.h include was first guarded on OPENDISPLAY_HAS_WIFI while the code using IP2STR sits
# under plain TARGET_ESP32. s3-n16r8 built fine; esp32-n4 -- the one board that compiles no WiFi
# surface at all -- did not. The guard is now TARGET_ESP32, and the lookup simply returns NULL
# on a board without WiFi, printing "?".

# 9 (UNCHANGED) = phase C step 9b-iii, 2026-08-04: src/wifi_service.cpp's LAN data path off
# WiFiServer/WiFiClient and onto lwIP descriptors, and src/main.h off <WiFi.h> entirely.
#
# THE COUNT DOES NOT MOVE AND THAT IS CORRECT. wifi_service.cpp still includes <Arduino.h> and
# <ESPmDNS.h>, so it is still a shim user; it leaves at step 9b-iv. main.h was already counted
# for its other shim includes. What this step does move is the SHIM ITSELF: compat/WiFi.h is
# DELETED -- the first shim header to go rather than shrink. Nothing in targets/ or third_party/
# includes it.
#
# Two file-static ints replace the two Arduino objects. WiFiServer/WiFiClient were already lwIP
# sockets underneath; the classes added an object identity this file never needed, because there
# is exactly one listener and at most one peer BY DESIGN (R3 refuses a second client rather than
# evicting the incumbent). The globals lived in main.h only because that is where this
# firmware's globals live -- nothing outside wifi_service.cpp ever touched them.
#
# ONE REAL DEFECT FOUND, in startLanServer(). Arduino's WiFiServer::begin() returns early when a
# socket already exists, so restartWiFiLanAfterReconnect() -- which runs after a WiFi config
# change and after a reconnect -- rebound nothing. Switching encryption on or off moves the LAN
# port (plaintext base, TLS base+1), so the device went on listening on the OLD port while
# logging the new one, until a reboot. startLanServer() now closes the listener before binding.
#
# The mbedTLS BIO ctx is still a POINTER (&s_lanClientFd), not the fd by value, for the reason
# the old code passed &wifiClient: mbedTLS holds it for the life of the ssl context and the
# descriptor changes when one session ends and another is admitted.
#
# WL_* / wl_status_t went too -- Arduino's names for values this file both produces and
# consumes, and which nothing outside it ever saw. Now OD_WIFI_*, same numeric values so a log
# line still compares the same against old firmware.
#
# NOT hardware-verified. This is the LAN DATA PATH: accept, TLS handshake BIO, frame reads and
# frame writes all changed at once, and a LAN regression is invisible on a BLE-only bench --
# every board here builds and advertises identically with LAN completely broken. Needs a real
# host push over both plaintext and TLS-PSK, a second client refused while one is connected,
# and a config change that flips encryption (to exercise the rebind above).

# 8 = phase C step 9b-iv, 2026-08-04: src/wifi_service.cpp and src/main.h off <ESPmDNS.h> and
# <Arduino.h>. compat/ESPmDNS.h DELETED. wifi_service.cpp -- the largest file in the WiFi
# group and the last of it -- finally leaves the count. 9 -> 8, one file.
#
# THE SHIM'S MDNSResponder WAS ENTIRELY NO-OPS. begin() returned true, addService() and
# addServiceTxt() did nothing whatsoever. This target has therefore never advertised anything
# since the import, while logging nine TXT records as though it had. The shim was honest about
# it -- "if the mdns component is not present in the build, these become no-ops rather than a
# build failure" -- and it was not present, because ESP-IDF dropped mdns from core in v5.0.
# Same shape as 9b-ii's WiFiClass, and the same lesson: a no-op that compiles is
# indistinguishable from a feature that works.
#
# So this step needed a DEPENDENCY before it could need a translation. espressif/mdns 1.11.3 is
# vendored at targets/esp32-idf/components/mdns in its own commit, configured advertise-only
# (this device announces; it never resolves or browses). Provenance in third_party/NOTICE.md,
# configuration and its reasoning in sdkconfig.defaults.
#
# THE UNDERSCORES ARE THE TRAP. Arduino's ESPmDNS took "opendisplay"/"tcp" and prefixed them
# itself; the IDF API takes "_opendisplay"/"_tcp" as written. Carrying the Arduino spelling
# across would have advertised `opendisplay.tcp.local` -- a name no client looks for, failing
# exactly like mDNS not working at all, which is the state we are leaving.
#
# Three behaviour changes beyond the translation, each because the target is now real:
#   - The six identity/capability TXT keys are registered as ONE array at add time, not as six
#     follow-up calls. Against a real responder the latter is six record mutations and up to
#     six re-announcements for a record never once advertised complete.
#   - restartLanService() replaces the service record rather than appending. The port MOVES
#     when encryption is toggled (plaintext base, TLS base+1).
#   - opendisplay_lan_teardown() now stops the responder, so a reboot sends a goodbye. The
#     shim's end() was a no-op like everything else, so clients kept a cached record pointing
#     at a rebooting device and retried the port through the whole restart.
#
# The 400 ms floor on the msd TXT update was already there and now MATTERS: every TXT change
# re-announces on the multicast group, and this device shares its antenna with BLE.
#
# Arduino String went with it (deviceName, mac) and so did millis()/delay() -> od_hal_time,
# which is what actually drops the <Arduino.h> include.
#
# Cost: +33,648 bytes of flash on each S3 board. The five non-WiFi boards are BYTE-IDENTICAL,
# confirming the linker discards the component where main never references it.
#
# NOT hardware-verified, and unusually here the bench test is easy and worth doing FIRST:
# `avahi-browse -r _opendisplay._tcp` on the LAN should show the device, its port, and all
# seven TXT keys. Nothing about this has ever worked on this target, so there is no regression
# risk -- only the question of whether the advertisement is correct.

# 7 = phase C step 11, 2026-08-04: src/main.h -- <Arduino.h>, <SPI.h> and <Wire.h> all three.
# 8 -> 7, one file. (Step 10, src/boot_screen.cpp, is deliberately still open: its include is
# vestigial and it can go at any time.)
#
# MAIN.H USED NONE OF THEM. It is the header that defines this firmware's globals, and it has
# no Arduino type or call of its own -- the three includes were serving main.cpp, its ONLY
# consumer, transitively. That is worth stating because it is the honest version of a decrement
# the ratchet cannot distinguish from laundering by itself: the surface did not move to another
# file, it was never used here.
#
# main.cpp then failed to compile on three call sites it had been getting through main.h, which
# is the whole reason the include had to be named rather than deleted:
#   - TWO Wire.end() -> od_hal_i2c_deinit(). compat/Wire.h's end() IS exactly that call, so this
#     is a real removal, not a rename.
#   - ONE SPI.end() -> stays, and main.cpp now includes <SPI.h> explicitly. It is NOT a one-line
#     HAL call: compat/SPI.h's end() releases the device AND frees the bus using ownership state
#     (_owns_bus) that only the shim holds, and there is no od_hal_spi. Naming the include costs
#     nothing on the count -- main.cpp is already counted for <Arduino.h> -- and hiding it in a
#     header would have been the laundering this file exists to catch.
#
# CHECKED FOR A BACK DOOR: main.h still includes <bb_epaper.h>, which is itself on the
# third_party shim list. It includes <Arduino.h> only under #ifdef ARDUINO, and this build does
# not define ARDUINO (it takes the __LINUX__ branch -- see main/CMakeLists.txt on why defining
# ARDUINO was rejected). So nothing is laundered in through it.
#
# All 10 images are BYTE-IDENTICAL to the previous commit. Expected, and the point: Wire.end()
# already compiled to od_hal_i2c_deinit(), so this step changes what the source DEPENDS on
# without changing what it generates. Nothing here needs hardware verification.
7
